<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Xavier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1506">1506</a>–<a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Francis Xavier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Xavier was a Navarrese priest and co-founder of the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> who became the leading Catholic missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Based in Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from 1542, he preached in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, and in 1549 landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where lords such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Otomo Sorin</a> received him. He died on an island off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1552 and was canonised in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-xavier.jpg" alt="Francis Xavier" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franciscus_de_Xabier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Portuguese chapel with a graveyard stood before 1542, when <b>Francis Xavier</b> visited the town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Portuguese traders who landed on Tanegashima in 1543 introduced the arquebus, which was rapidly copied by Japanese gunsmiths and transformed the warfare of the Sengoku period, while the Jesuit mission launched by <b>Francis Xavier</b> in 1549 won hundreds of thousands of converts before the Tokugawa suppression of Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Francis Xavier</b> Pierz, the local historian Fr.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jesuit missionary <b>Francis Xavier</b> died of fever on Shangchuan Island while waiting to enter mainland China.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-3">3 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ignatius of Loyola, who with six companions had founded the Society of Jesus and was its first Superior General, was canonized together with <b>Francis Xavier</b>, the Jesuit co-founder who led the first Christian mission to Japan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Funai traded with Ming China and with the Portuguese, and Sorin welcomed <b>Francis Xavier</b> in 1551, protected Jesuit missionaries and was baptised in 1578.</blockquote><a href="/state/otomo-clan-domain">Otomo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
